WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.559 --> 00:00:01.759
- Everyone's looking.

00:00:01.759 --> 00:00:03.260
It's fine okay.

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:05.171
And then it starts to get a little, right?

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:06.004
And then you--

00:00:06.004 --> 00:00:06.941
Nope it got worse.

00:00:06.941 --> 00:00:09.024
It got worse on that one.

00:00:11.685 --> 00:00:12.731
So we're both coffee lovers.

00:00:12.731 --> 00:00:14.349
- And we've always wanted to
learn how to make latte art.

00:00:14.349 --> 00:00:16.217
- So today we're headed down
to Stumptown Coffee Roasters

00:00:16.217 --> 00:00:17.972
in New York City to learn how.

00:00:17.972 --> 00:00:19.526
- I'm Susie Locklier.

00:00:19.526 --> 00:00:20.878
I work for Stumptown Coffee Roasters

00:00:20.878 --> 00:00:22.884
and I've been a barista
for over eight years now.

00:00:22.884 --> 00:00:25.257
The plan is to teach
you guys some latte art.

00:00:25.257 --> 00:00:26.986
There are a couple of fundamental things

00:00:26.986 --> 00:00:30.117
that go into making a beautiful latte.

00:00:30.117 --> 00:00:31.398
So today I'm gonna teach you

00:00:31.398 --> 00:00:33.507
how to pour a heart into a latte,

00:00:33.507 --> 00:00:35.638
which is a fundamental design to start out

00:00:35.638 --> 00:00:37.494
sort of your latte art journey.

00:00:37.494 --> 00:00:40.153
And that entails just keeping
your cup nice and tilted,

00:00:40.153 --> 00:00:43.553
you're gonna pour that milk
into that deep pool of espresso

00:00:43.553 --> 00:00:45.376
and then you come down
not moving very much

00:00:45.376 --> 00:00:47.707
but the heart sort of
blooms into your cup.

00:00:47.707 --> 00:00:49.286
So we're gonna go through
the steps of that.

00:00:49.286 --> 00:00:51.279
So just go ahead and pour
the milk into the pitcher,

00:00:51.279 --> 00:00:53.026
right below that line.

00:00:53.026 --> 00:00:54.486
- I'm fillin' this up.

00:00:54.486 --> 00:00:56.707
- So there are two main
parts of steaming milk:

00:00:56.707 --> 00:00:57.746
stretching the milk,

00:00:57.746 --> 00:00:58.827
which is introducing air,

00:00:58.827 --> 00:01:00.107
which is that sound that you hear,

00:01:00.107 --> 00:01:01.558
that (imitates steam escaping) sound.

00:01:01.558 --> 00:01:04.598
And that's what takes your
cold sort of flat milk

00:01:04.598 --> 00:01:07.717
and turns it into nice
creamy textured latte milk.

00:01:07.717 --> 00:01:08.550
- Okay.

00:01:11.417 --> 00:01:12.250
Once.

00:01:13.118 --> 00:01:16.297
- And then grab the milk
pitcher and give it a nice spin

00:01:16.297 --> 00:01:18.046
because milk separates by density

00:01:18.046 --> 00:01:19.656
so the foam is gonna float to the top

00:01:19.656 --> 00:01:21.837
and the liquid is gonna
sink to the bottom.

00:01:21.837 --> 00:01:24.685
Tilt our cup here pitcher-distance,

00:01:24.685 --> 00:01:26.376
right for the center.

00:01:26.376 --> 00:01:29.067
- Oh that was terrible!

00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:31.056
That doesn't even look like a heart!

00:01:31.056 --> 00:01:31.889
- And all the way through.

00:01:31.889 --> 00:01:32.722
- Oh!

00:01:32.722 --> 00:01:33.555
- Beautiful, set that down on the counter.

00:01:33.555 --> 00:01:34.880
- That's cute!

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:35.713
- Oh yay!

00:01:35.713 --> 00:01:36.546
So that's beautiful.

00:01:36.546 --> 00:01:37.555
You have nice contrast.

00:01:37.555 --> 00:01:38.388
Great job!

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.036
Maggie, you did really
well on your first try,

00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:44.335
so now you're gonna go solo.

00:01:44.335 --> 00:01:46.547
We're gonna try again.

00:01:46.547 --> 00:01:47.466
Nice!

00:01:47.466 --> 00:01:48.598
- Nope it got worse.

00:01:48.598 --> 00:01:50.067
It got worse on that one.

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:53.177
- So we've made an improvement
from the last one, right?

00:01:53.177 --> 00:01:55.117
Because you actually
can see a little heart!

00:01:55.117 --> 00:01:55.950
- [Erin] Yeah!

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:56.816
- [Susie] Do you see it?

00:01:56.816 --> 00:01:58.115
- So we tried our best,

00:01:58.115 --> 00:02:00.586
but I think we should leave
this up to the professionals.

00:02:00.586 --> 00:02:02.551
Can you show us some
of your other designs?

00:02:02.551 --> 00:02:03.585
- Yeah, let's do it!

00:02:03.585 --> 00:02:05.038
So first I'm gonna do a tulip.

00:02:05.038 --> 00:02:05.956
That's what I'm the best at.

00:02:05.956 --> 00:02:07.284
It's my tried and true design.

00:02:07.284 --> 00:02:10.978
So you can't have good latte
art without good coffee

00:02:10.978 --> 00:02:13.878
'cause you need crema to
float your milk on top of

00:02:13.878 --> 00:02:17.227
and you also can't have latte
art without well-steamed milk.

00:02:17.227 --> 00:02:19.845
So they kinda work in
conjunction with one another

00:02:19.845 --> 00:02:22.198
to make you know your beautiful latte.

00:02:22.198 --> 00:02:23.646
Next I'm gonna do a heart.

00:02:23.646 --> 00:02:25.397
What really drew me to coffee was

00:02:25.397 --> 00:02:27.067
the collaborative nature of it.

00:02:27.067 --> 00:02:29.176
Coffee is hand-picked by one person.

00:02:29.176 --> 00:02:30.595
It passes through a ton of hands before

00:02:30.595 --> 00:02:31.827
it actually gets to the barista

00:02:31.827 --> 00:02:34.437
and that sort of just
harbors a lot of community.

00:02:34.437 --> 00:02:37.686
It harbors connection between people.

00:02:37.686 --> 00:02:40.598
The last design I'm going
to pour is The Rosetta,

00:02:40.598 --> 00:02:43.775
which is my nemesis
because there's sort of

00:02:43.775 --> 00:02:46.227
two sides of the brain: Tulip and Rosetta.

00:02:46.227 --> 00:02:48.296
It just calls on different skills.

00:02:48.296 --> 00:02:50.758
So the most important
thing that I tell baristas

00:02:50.758 --> 00:02:53.747
about latte art is that it
doesn't taste like anything.

00:02:53.747 --> 00:02:55.856
It's half of the equation,

00:02:55.856 --> 00:02:57.998
but people eat with their eyes,

00:02:57.998 --> 00:02:59.926
so you have to have well-steamed milk,

00:02:59.926 --> 00:03:01.246
well-extracted espresso,

00:03:01.246 --> 00:03:06.163
and a love for the craft to
kind of have the whole package.

00:03:08.992 --> 00:03:13.457
- Special thanks to Stumptown
Coffee for having us!

00:03:13.457 --> 00:03:17.124
(dramatic orchestral music)

